EXT. THE GLASS DESERT - DAWN

A vast plain of fused sand, smooth as a frozen sea. Wind carves ripples in the surface. The sky is a bruise of peach and violet. Far off, a ridge of black towers rises like broken teeth.

Two figures stand on the glass. Each throws a long shadow.

THE WARDEN OF SALT, tall, gaunt, draped in a cloak of white crystals, holds a staff topped with a clouded lens. Where her feet touch the glass, small stars of frost bloom.

OPPOSITE HER, a girl: WILHELMINA "WILLA" FAIRWEATHER-ACHEBE, 15, in a patched travelling coat, a leather satchel across her chest. She carries a lantern whose flame is a deep, steady blue.

WARDEN
You are a long way from the green.

WILLA
I had a reason.

WARDEN
Everyone does.

WILLA
Mine is better.

The Warden tilts her head. The crystals on her cloak chime.

WARDEN
Show me.

Willa lifts the lantern. The blue flame flares. Across the glass, a hundred feet away, a patch of sand ripples and darkens, as though something beneath it has turned over in its sleep.

WARDEN (CONT'D)
(a long breath)
Who gave you that?

WILLA
My grandmother.

WARDEN
Where is she?

WILLA
Under the sand.

WARDEN
I do not take the dead.

WILLA
She said you'd say that.

The Warden raises her staff. The lens clears. In it, a swirl of tiny figures: a woman in a shawl, a child on her knee, an old man laughing, all spinning slowly.

WILLA (CONT'D)
(whispering)
That's her.

WARDEN
So it is.

WILLA
Let her go.

WARDEN
She is not mine to release.

WILLA
Then whose?

The Warden looks past the girl at the black towers. Wind rattles the crystals on her cloak.

WARDEN
Theirs.

WILLA
The Silent Court.

WARDEN
You know the name.

WILLA
I know all the names. She taught me.

Willa steps forward. The lantern's blue light spills across the glass, and where it touches, cracks race out in glittering fans. The Warden stumbles back a half step.

WARDEN
Careful. This ground is old.

WILLA
So am I.

WARDEN
You are fifteen.

WILLA
I've been fifteen a long time.

They stand. The wind drops. Even the cracks stop whispering.

WARDEN
What will you give?

WILLA
What do you ask?

WARDEN
A season of your life.

WILLA
Which season?

WARDEN
Spring.

Willa's face does not change. Her hand tightens on the lantern.

WILLA
Why spring?

WARDEN
Because it is when you were meant to bloom.

WILLA
And in exchange?

WARDEN
One hour with her. No more.

WILLA
An hour.

WARDEN
Under the sand. Where she sleeps.

Willa looks at the blue flame. At the towers. At the lens, where a tiny woman raises her small hand.

WILLA
(to herself)
One hour.

WARDEN
Choose.

WILLA
I choose.

WARDEN
Speak it.

At the same moment, in the same breath, both of them whisper a single word.

WILLA
Yes.

WARDEN ^
Yes.

The glass shudders. A wide circle of the desert floor turns clear as water, and beneath it, deep down, a long stair descends into lamplight.

The Warden steps aside. She sweeps one crystalled arm toward the opening. Willa walks forward, lantern held before her. Frost blooms behind her heels.

At the edge of the clear circle she pauses. Does not turn.

WILLA (CONT'D)
Warden. Will it hurt?

WARDEN
Only when you climb back up.

Willa nods. Takes the first step down. The Warden stands alone on the glass, her staff planted, the wind picking up again, until the girl's blue light shrinks to a point and winks out.
